South African doctor: Omikron has a strong transmissibility, but it is mostly mild
A number of doctors and medical experts in South Africa believe that the Omikjon variant of the new coronavirus is milder, although it spreads more rapidly.
According to the Associated Press, with the spread of the Omikron variant in South Africa, more than 5,000 doctors in Gauteng, South Africa's Gauteng province, reported that the new crown patients they are currently treating have relatively mild symptoms, such as physical pain and cough, similar to flu symptoms.
The organization’s superintendent, Dr. Unben Pillay, said he saw dozens of patients every day, but none of them needed to be hospitalized.
“They were able to control the disease at home, and most people recover within 10 to 14 days of isolation,” Pillay said.
This includes elderly patients and those who have health problems, who would have been more likely to get into severe illness due to coronavirus infection, he said.
In contrast, he said, the new crown patient he was treating had “difficulty breathing and had low blood oxygen levels,” he said.
A lot of people are going to be hospitalized within a few days.”
Other doctors shared similar experiences within two weeks of being first reported in Southern Africa.
While it’s still a matter of weeks to gather enough data, doctors’ observations and early evidence provide some clues.
According to South Africa's National Institute of Infectious Diseases, only 30% of new crown patients hospitalized in the past few weeks are seriously ill, which is half of the severity of the previous epidemic; the average length of hospitalization has been shortened from 8 days to 2.8 days; only 3% of recent new crown patients have died, and the proportion of deaths in early outbreaks in South Africa has reached 20%.
Willem Hanekom, director of the African Health Research Institute, cited data from the National Institute of Infectious Diseases and other reports: â€œFor now, almost all the signs indicate that this is a milder disease.â€
“It’s still an early stage, and we need to get late data.
"Normally, hospitalizations and deaths happen late, and we're only two weeks into the epidemic."
At the same time, scientists around the world are looking at the number of cases and hospitalizations while testing the effectiveness of current vaccines and treatments.
Although Delta remains the world’s leading new coronavirus strain, Omikron cases are emerging in dozens of countries, with South Africa at the epicentre of the outbreak.
According to health officials, the number of new cases detected in Gauteng province increased by 400% in the first week of December, and tests showed more than 90 percent of the Omikron variant.
