The International Olive Oil Association classifies virgin olive oil (Virgin olive oil) and refined olive oil (Lampante Olive Oil or Refined Olive Oil).
Virgin olive oil, also known as natural olive oil, is taken directly from the fresh olive fruit by being mechanically squeezed, filtered and treated to remove the foreign body.
It can be divided into three levels according to acidity:
Extra virgin olive oil is the highest quality olive oil and is an all-natured product.
The taste is excellent, there is a fragrant fragrant plant, and the acidity is not more than 1%.
High-quality virgin olive oil (Fine Virgin): a slightly higher acidity, but not more than 2%, the taste is pure and aromatic.
Ordinary olive oil (Ordinary Virgin): the taste and flavor are good, and the acidity is not more than 3.3%.
Refined olive oil refers to olive oil obtained after the refining of virgin olive oil with an acid degree of more than 3.3%, or becomes a "secondary oil".
Refined olive oil can be divided into two levels:
Ordinary olive oil (Olive oil): Refined olive oil is mixed with a certain proportion of virgin olive oil to reconcile the taste and color, with an acidity of less than 1.5% and a transparent golden yellow color.
Refined olive impurity oil, or olive-Pomace oil (Refined olive-Pomace oil), obtained by disintegration and refined from linoleum.
If you’ve looked at the classification, you’ve probably found that the acidity of olive oil is a very important indicator.
You should know that olives are like grapes that make wine, olives from different production regions and different olive varieties will produce different standards of olive oil.
The best places to grow olives:
The Mediterranean coast has the most suitable climatic conditions such as soil and temperature, sunlight, air humidity and precipitation required for the growth of oleaginous olives.
With the exception of the shores of the Mediterranean, other areas can only be grown sporadically.
Main production areas (Mediterranean):
Italy, Spain, Greece, Turkey, Portugal, Morocco, Syria, Israel, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Pakistan, Lebanon and so on, similar to their climate are similar to Argentina, Peru, South Africa and so on.
Months of olive picking:
From March to June of each year, it is the time when the olive tree blooms, and the fruits begin to develop and mature from July to October, and the fruit transitions from green to red, purple, and finally black.
From November to February of the following year is the picking season of the fruit (the freshness of the olive oil is very important).
Nutrients of olive oil:
Olive oil is rich in mono-unsaturated fatty acids, such as linoleic acid and linoleic acid and linoleic acid, linoleic acid, as well as vitamin A, vitamin B, vitamin D, vitamin E, vitamin K and antioxidants.
Olive oil is believed to be one of the oils found so far that is most suitable for human nutrition.
At the end of the democracy summit, Biden said that the fire of freedom will never be extinguished.
The online Summit for Democracy, convened by U.S.S. President Joe Biden and attended by leaders of 110 governments around the world, as well as civil society and the private-sector, ended Friday.
In his closing remarks, Biden said that the fire of freedom burns in the hearts of everyone in the world, and authoritarian regimes will never be extinguished.
He also said he plans to hold the second global "Democracy Summit" next year.
Taiwan government representative and government councilor Tang Feng and Hong Kong democracy and human rights activist Luo Guancong, who is currently in exile in the United Kingdom, also spoke at the next day's summit.
Biden said, "We know how hard the work ahead of us is.
“But we also know that we can rise to the challenge, because I’ve said it before, as this summit shows, the democratic world is everywhere.
An autocratic regime can never quench the flame of freedom, it burns in the hearts of people all over the world and all over the world.”
Biden also said that the defense of democracy requires the joint efforts of society as a whole.
“As the leaders of our government, we have a responsibility to listen to our citizens, harden the guardrails of democracy, drive reforms, achieve transparent and accountable governance that is more resilient to autocratic buffers and shocks, as well as those who want to pursue power outright before the public good,” he said.
The theme of the second day of the Democracy Summit is to defend human rights, consolidate democracy and confront authoritarianism, as well as to counter digital authoritarianism and affirm democratic values.
This day coincides with "International Human Rights Day".
United Nations Secretary-General António Guterres also said in his opening speech on the same day that the cause of democracy faces challenges around the world, especially the challenges posed by emerging digital technologies.
“It’s time to reaffirm our shared values and positions and democratic resilience,” he said.
This requires deepening social dialogue, tackling inequality, fighting corruption, renewing the social contract, investing in quality education, and expanding opportunities for women and girls.
It will also require a strong condemnation of populism, nativism, and extremism in all its forms, including the exploitation of all the tools offered by the technological revolution.”
Hong Kong democracy and human rights activist Luo Guancong spoke to the summit through a pre-recorded video to remind the participants to continue to pay attention to the human rights situation in Hong Kong and Chinese minorities such as Uighurs and Tibetans.
“For too long, the world has embraced China’s rise without establishing a mechanism to hold it accountable,” he said.
That’s why politicians and civil society leaders must work together.
“We must use everything we have (resources) to make sure that the revival of democracy is our top priority.”
Luo also called on the free world to unite against the CCP’s threats.
“Maybe some of you are afraid to annoy General Secretary Xi Jinping, maybe some of you don’t want to lose the Chinese market, and maybe some of you don’t recognize the threat to our democratic values,” he said.
And that’s the reason for our failure.
That’s why we have to change.”
In a round-table discussion on combating digital authoritarianism and affirming the values of democracy, Taiwan's administrative staff member Tang Feng released the "State Statement" through a video, emphasizing that Taiwan is at the forefront of the fight against authoritarianism and authoritarianism.
He also shared the experience of combating the new crown epidemic through information transparency and building mutual trust.
"At a time when Taiwan's democracy is on the rise, we have always stood firmly at the forefront of the global fight against authoritarianism and played a leading role in promoting global freedom, democracy, and human rights," he said.
During the epidemic, authoritarian governments everywhere justified human rights violations in the name of public health and collective interests, and global democracy showed signs of retrogression.
However, in Taiwan, we don't have to close the city to control the epidemic, we don't have to take off the shelves to defeat disinformation."
At the same time as the second day of the Democracy Summit, the U.S. Treasury Department announced that 15 individuals and 10 entities in several countries, such as China and North Korea, were found to have committed serious violations of human rights, and imposed investment restrictions on a Chinese company found to use surveillance technology to help violate human rights.
