Việt Nam People's Daily - Gulf Cooperation Council tackles common challenges
Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman’s stops include Oman, the United Arab Emirates (UAE), Qatar, Bahrain and Kuwait.
It’s worth noting that it’s the first time Crown Prince Mohammed has visited Qatar since Saudi Arabia and its Arab allies imposed an embargo on Qatar in mid-2017, leading to a diplomatic crisis in the Gulf.
During the crisis, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Egypt severed their diplomatic relations with Qatar and imposed a comprehensive embargo on it, accusing Qatar of supporting terrorist organizations and having close ties with Iran.
Diplomatual tensions have severely affected the GCC’s internal unity and have had a negative impact on the economies of the countries in the region.
Four Arab countries have blocked land, sea and air routes to Qatar.
The crisis was resolved only at the Gulf Cooperation Council summit in January 2021, when the Gulf states signed an agreement mediated by the United States and Kuwait to move toward “unity and stability.”
Saudi Arabia has re-opened its border with Qatar.
Saudi Arabia and Egypt are spearheading efforts to restore relations with Qatar by appointing a new ambassador between the two countries.
The Gulf Arab states resolve their differences in order to restore their relations with Qatar out of the need to unite for the interests of all countries and regional stability, in order to address common challenges.
During the Saudi crown prince's visit to Oman, the two sides agreed to open the first direct land route between the two Gulf neighbors.
The 725-kilometer-long corridor connecting the two countries will facilitate the travel of citizens of the two countries and the integration of supply chains.
Saudi Arabia’s public investment fund (PIF) will invest $5 billion in Oman, and companies from the two countries have signed 13 memoranda of understanding worth $30 billion.
The Saudi crown prince’s visit to the Gulf states coincides with negotiations between Iran and world powers to salvage the Iran nuclear deal known as the JCPOA (JCPOA), an agreement that Gulf Arab states have denouncated for not being able to resolve the missile issue with Iran.
The Gulf states have always regarded Iran as a long-term “competitor” and a “threat” to regional security.
The Gulf states see no solution to Iran’s missile problem and not taking action to curb Iran’s activities in the region.
In the context of nuclear negotiations between the major powers and Iran that failed to meet the wishes of the various Gulf countries, Saudi Arabia has conducted direct negotiations with Iran.
Its goal is to contain the escalation of tensions in the region.
This is one of the factors that has slowed economic growth in the Gulf Cooperation Council countries.
The upcoming GCC summit will be the first by GCC countries since the end of the dispute between some member states and Qatar.
This is an opportunity for the Gulf Cooperation Council to re-evaluate the diplomatic achievements that have contributed to the restoration of the GCC unity, while proposing measures to enable its member States to cooperate fully in order to address common challenges and contribute to the stability and development of the Gulf States.
