Asian-American group complains about discrimination in enrollment at 3 famous Ivy schools such as Yale - Xinhua
WASHINGTON, May 23 (Xinhua) -- More than 130 U.S. Asian-American groups have filed complaints with the U.S. Department of Education and the Department of Justice on the 23rd, protesting the discrimination against Asian-American applicants in three leading Ivy League schools, Yale University and Dartmouth.
The Asian-American Education Alliance, which spearheaded the appeal, held a press conference in Washington on the same day.
In the past 10 years, the number of Asian-American applicants who meet the admission requirements of U.S. universities has increased exponentially, but the proportion of Asians in these three U.S. universities has basically remained at the same level.
Many studies have shown that these colleges and universities are "consistent and systematically discripetally discriminating against Asian-American applicants" in the so-called "comprehensive consideration" admissions process.
In 2014, research by Sanders and other scholars showed that in these three colleges and universities, Asians had the lowest acceptance rate among all ethnic groups relative to the number of applicants.
Data from the U.S. Department of Education also shows that of all Ivy League schools, Brown University and Dartmouth College have the lowest acceptance rate of Asian-American students, and Yale and Brown universities have implemented a de facto racial quota for Asian-American applicants for more than 20 years.
Zhao told Xinhua that they expect the U.S. Departments of Education and the Justice Department to launch an investigation and take steps to prevent these universities from continuing to discriminating against Asian-Americans in their admissions after finding the facts.
They also look forward to the colleges and universities that have complained of taking the initiative to pay attention to this issue and change the admissions policy for Asian Americans.
At present, the three universities in the complaint have not yet responded.
Founded in 2015, the Asian-American Education Alliance filed a complaint about discrimination in admissions at Harvard, but the U.S. Department of Education did not accept it on procedural grounds.
In addition to the Chinese-American groups involved in the complaint, it also included educational groups of Indian, Korean, Japanese, and Pakistani.
