3,200 newborn babies in Sabah are cut off without newspapers due to special anti-counterfeiting paper
The Sabah National Registration Bureau said that due to the use of special anti-counterfeiting paper inventory, it has not been possible since the beginning of October to issue birth papers to newborn babies, and 3,200 people have been affected so far.
However, the agency’s director, Kairu Aminus, assured that for all the babies who have applied for birth papers, the data has been recorded in the system and will be reissued when the anti-counterfeiting paper is shipped next week.
At the press conference, he gave an explanation in response to an online media report that there had been a 10-month-long report on the printing of paper in Sabah.
He said that the problem arose two months ago, and Sarawak faced the same problem.
"One of the reasons is the production of special anti-counterfeiting paper Chinese manufacturers affected by the epidemic, resulting in the delayed arrival in our country."
He explained that anti-counterfeiting paper used in Sabah paper is different from that of other regions, so it is not possible to borrow inventory from other states.
Kairu Aminus revealed that the latest batch of anti-counterfeiting paper has arrived in Kuala Lumpur on the 6th of this month, after which it was handed over to the printing company on the 8th to carry out partial printing work.
â€œThe bureau will send people to Kuala Lumpur next Thursday to pick it up and go back to Sabah to print the final stage, and it is estimated that more than two working days (December 20) can be handed over to the applicant within an additional two working days.”
"We will notify the applicant to go to the local bureau to receive the paper."
Sabah has issued a total of 24,801 papers so far this year.
