When visiting the book market, you can often feel the popularity of successful learning books.
Many of today’s successful books promote “the best principles”—that is, presupposing or implying that you can â€œdoneâ€ and then telling you â€œwhat â€œwhat needs to be doneâ€
It's the same as going through the "how to do it", which is actually the most important and most fundamental link.
Because of this, these books have a common feature - either they do their best, or they don't do anything at all; they are either 100 or 0, or you can climb Mount Tai, or you just stay where they are.
This â€œbest principleâ€ allows anyone to admit that â€œthe right thingâ€ , but 99% of people will be intimidated in front of the starting line.
Because this kind of theory promotes a path of "you have to make up your mind, re-humanize, and have a 180-degree transformation", which is the fatal weakness of a lot of people.
For those who follow the rules, follow the rules, and refuse to change, you have to shout "you want to change" in his ears, which is undoubtedly self-defeating and playing the harp on cattle.
The vast majority of people in front of the "transformation" in front of timid, trapped, difficult to make a decision; or with a momentary impulse to sow the determination of "transformation", and bravely took the first step, but it is often not a long-satisfied, unable to stick to a long time to lose, difficult to see the continuity.
Mr. Ma Sanli's well-known "starting tomorrow" sound is the most evangelical manifestation of this "universal human nature" classic.
The biggest problem of the "best principle" is that the threshold is too high, it often exceeds the limits of the ability of ordinary people, so that the vast majority of people "look forward to sigh".
Perhaps the "principle principle" is a way to put more ordinary people on the path of relative success.
At the core of the â€œprinciple â€œreduce the thresholdâ€ â€“ it encourages the theory of â€œaccumulating small wins to big wins â€œthat if you canâ€œcan't do 100, you can only do 1 if you don't do it: because 1 is always greater than 0; if you don't go to Taishan, you can only climb the small sand dunes on your doorstep.
In short, as long as you do something, it’s better than not.
Because the threshold is greatly reduced, there are more possibilities for ordinary people to persist, and as long as everything persists, the probability of success will also increase.
The road to success in accordance with the "principle" is only a little longer.
All in all, the â€œbest principlesâ€ ostensibly under the guise of success for all, but it can eventually inspire, in fact, a small number; and the â€œprincipleâ€ can effectively motivate more people and benefit as much as possible.
