Metaphysics refers to the study, through rational reasoning and logic, questions that cannot be answered directly through perception.
Metaphysics is the fundamental law of philosophy designed to explain the fundamental nature of existence and the world.
The Chinese translation of "metaphysics" is derived from the words "I Ching • Speech Upload" and "the way of the metaphysical, the metaphysic is called the instrument", which is translated from Metaphysic in the Japanese Inoue (the Meiji era).
The metaphysical branches include ontology, natural theology, and the question of its extension.
Ontology refers to “the study of which nouns represent the real existing entity, and which nouns only represent a kind of concept.”
Metaphysical naturalism refers to the denial of God’s existence.
Its theoretical basis is that all phenomena can be explained in terms of the concept of natural reasons, proposed by the philosophers who support science and evolution.
Naturalism, on the other hand, refers to the study of God without a word.
Spiritual philosophy is a branch of philosophy that studies the nature of consciousness and its relationship to the body (especially the brain).
Dualism and monism are the two main ideas for discussing the “relationship between mind and body.”
Epistemology is a branch of philosophy that explores the nature, origin and scope of knowledge.
Ethic is the discipline of systematic thinking and research on the moral life of human beings; in this case, morality is defined as all the codes of conduct recognized by a group of people or a culture.
It is mainly divided into meta-ethics (absplicative study of the nature of ethical theory or criticism), normative ethics (judging a variety of different kinds of ethics, and giving moral guidelines for correct or wrong behavior).
It investigates the manner in which the moral code of conduct should be observed, applied ethics (academic application of ethical theories to real-life situations, including bioethics, political ethics, etc.), describing ethics (collecting information about how people live, summarizing from the observed patterns).
Its examination of ethics does not start with the supposed theory, but investigates the real choices made and observed by the actual moral agents, which contributes to all the moral elements of this choice, and it deals with the ethics held by the social race.
The history of ethics includes Greco-Roman ethics and medieval Christian ethics.
Part I: The Ethical Idea of Humanism, represented by Walla and Montaigne, among others.
Part II: Ethical ideas of the Reformation, represented by Calvin.
Part III: The ethics of politics, represented by Machiavelli and others, modern ethics (17th-18th century, British empiricist ethics, representatives: Hume et al., 17th and 18th centuries, continental rationalist ethics, representatives: Spinoza et al., 18th century, French Enlightenment ethics, representative figures: Voltaire, 18th-19th-century, German academic ethics, representative figures, representative figures: Conde and Hegel, 18th-19th century, English utilitarian ethics, 18th and 19th century, British utilitarian ethics, 18th-19th century, British utilitarian ethics, 18th-19th century, British utilitarian ethics, 18th and 18th-19th century, British utilitarian ethics, representative of the 18th century.
Logic is the study of “principles and standards of effective inference and proof.”
Logic usually consists of three parts: inductive reasoning, attributive reasoning, and deductive reasoning, which can be divided into formal and non-formal logic.
Aesthetics is the study of the nature of beauty and its significance.
