Sino-Saudi Curators Collaborate to Hold Saudi Arabia's First Contemporary Art Biennale - Xinhua
RIYADH, Dec. 12 (Xinhua) -- Saudi Arabia's first Biennale of Contemporary Art â€“ the Diriere Contemporary Art Biennale â€“ was officially opened to the public on the 11th in the Di Rie JAX art district northwest of the Saudi capital Riyadh, with a total of 64 artists from Saudi Arabia, China and other places exhibiting.
The Biennale is jointly curated by the Saudi Diriye Biennale Foundation and the China UCCA Ullens Center for Contemporary Art, and Tian Xiaoyu, director of UCCA Ullens Center for Contemporary Art in China, serves as artistic director.
According to the curatorial team, the theme of the Biennale is â€œcrossing the river with stonesâ€, which is a metaphor for the reform strategy adopted during the period of socio-economic transformation, and also reflects the current situation of Saudi social and cultural development.
The Biennale covers an area of nearly 12,000 square meters, and the exhibition hall straddles six interconnected buildings and is presented in six units in six units: "crossing the river with the stone", "Inheritance as an experiment", "edge thinking", "going to the public", "Beautiful New World", and "On the spirit of art".
Among them, the number of works of 27 Saudi artists accounted for more than 40% of the total number of exhibits, including paintings, installation art and performing arts and other forms; the works of 12 Chinese artists are also of great concern, and some artists use recycled materials to create in a novel way, such as Xu Bing reconstructed a classic landscape painting with waste in the endless map of Xishan in the "Strength story series behind it" (2014).
Barkley, chief executive officer of the Dirieux Biennale Foundation, expressed the hope that the Biennale will establish an international dialogue that will give global audiences the opportunity to learn about Saudi Arabia’s burgeoning cultural industries and creative communities and enhance interaction.
â€œThe Biennale's goal is twofold, allowing Saudi contemporary art to participate in a global dialogue, as well as the integration of Saudi audiences into the contemporary art world.â€
It is understood that there will also be a series of lectures during the opening week of the Biennale, where artists, curators and opinion leaders of the creative industry will discuss topics such as cross-cultural exchanges and the development of contemporary art.
