In the past two years, the blockade and containment of Huawei's chips has not only plunged Huawei into an unprecedented predicament, but it has also suffered huge losses for the "American system" enterprises.
The United States suppresses China's chip industry and requires its own enterprises and relevant enterprises in allied countries to block China's chip industry at the same time, and the direct consequence is that the orders and profits of global chip suppliers have been affected, resulting in lower R&D investment and reduced production capacity of chip production enterprises, resulting in a shortage of chip supply chains around the world.
Now the "lack of core" has spread to the world's major industries, such as car companies discontinued production, mobile phone price increases, almost all of the industries that use chips are deeply affected.
In order to master the autonomy of "chips", more and more country's technology giants have begun to embark on the road of "de-glorification".
In order to firmly control the control of the global semiconductor industry and contain the development of China's semiconductor industry, the United States recently took frequent actions and presided over the "chip summit"; it also invited the 64-nation giants to set up a semiconductor alliance, and supported the U.S. local technology giants, for example, the old U.S. technology giant Intel in order to regain its dominant position in the field of chips, spent 20 billion U.S. dollars to build two wafer processing plants in the United States, and open to the chip foundry business.
When most people think that Intel's move will have a certain impact on TSMC, and it is likely to steal TSMC's "job".
The reason why it is so certain is that Intel, as a veteran technology giant of the United States, has been cultivated in the semiconductor field for several decades and has a large team of talents and technical reserves, which will surely rise rapidly in the short term to defeat TSMC.
However, for Intel's decision, TSMC's founder Zhang Zhongmou was dismissive.
At the "2021 master think tank forum" held in April of this year, Zhang Zhongmou said amazingly, for Intel's move to build a fab, Zhang Zhongmou had no scruples: Intel built a chip foundry, quite ironic.
The reason why Zhang is quite disdainful of Intel's entry into the field of wafers is that Zhang Zhongmou is still obsessed with TSMC and Intel more than 30 years ago.
In the sixties and seventies of the last century, Intel has always been a pearl in the manufacturing industry, ranking at the peak of the world, and it is the hegemon of the world's semiconductor field.
By the 1980s, the world's semiconductor high-end chips have been mastered by a small number of powerful people.
At that time, the semiconductor industry was characterized by the IDM model, and the chip was completed by a factory from design and manufacturing to sealing and testing, and Intel was the representative of that era.
However, such a model makes some small enterprises overwhelmed, and many semiconductor entrepreneurs can only be deterred in the face of huge production line construction costs.
Under the inspiration of market demand, after entering the 1980s, the chip industry began to have the phenomenon of division of labor and cooperation, in 1984 Zhang Zhongmou heard that there is a company that specializes in chip design, thinking that since there is a special design, then you can set up a factory dedicated to wafer manufacturing.
Not long after, Zhang Zhongmou's vision became a reality, and TSMC came into being.
TSMC's main business is aimed at the single link of "wafer processing", does not design, and only provides foundry services for wafer manufacturing.
TSMC, which had just been established at that time, was small in the face of Intel's Big Mac.
In order to obtain capital support, Zhang Zhongmou had sought investment from Intel, but at that time, as soon as Intel heard that TSMC was only doing manufacturing links in the chip industry, it was obvious that some self-esteem was very high, looked down on TSMC, and decisively refused to invest in TSMC.
Zhang Zhongmou also believes that Intel was looking down on wafer process services at that time.
I didn't expect the giants who looked down on wafer manufacturing services at that time, but now they also have to start doing wafer foundries.
Therefore, Zhang Zhongmou felt that it was ironic for Intel to enter the wafer foundry.
At present, the market capitalization of TSMC has exceeded that of Intel.
In the face of TSMC's strong attack, Intel naturally did not dare to stand still.
Today, TSMC has sprinted towards 3nm chips, and Intel is still stuck in 14nm seven years ago.
It’s not really an easy task to catch up.
